---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { release } from "../data/release";

const bootstrap = release.installCommand;
const upgrade = release.upgradeCommand;

const up = `sudo storm-server up
sudo storm-server status`;

const upCustom = `sudo storm-server up \\
  --data-root /srv/storm \\
  --vault-root /path/to/vaults \\
  --state /srv/storm/state`;

const pair = `sudo -u storm storm-server pair \\
  --state /srv/storm/state --qr`;

const accounts = `sudo -u storm storm-server user add <name> --state /srv/storm/state
sudo -u storm storm-server user list --state /srv/storm/state
sudo -u storm storm-server passwd <name> --state /srv/storm/state`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install"
  description="Install storm-server from the apt repository on Debian or Ubuntu, then download clients from GitHub Releases."
>
  <header class="page-hero wrap-narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">07 · Installation</p>
    <h1>Install Storm</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Preferred path: one bootstrap on Debian or Ubuntu, then
      <code>storm-server up</code>. The package ships the binary, systemd unit,
      and web client. Then
      <a href="/clients">get a client</a>
      for your device.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="linux">
    <h2>Linux · apt</h2>
    <p class="support">
      The script adds Storm’s apt key and source, then runs
      <code>apt install storm-server</code>. Served from the apt repo root at
      <a href={release.aptRoot} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >dewanshdt.github.io/Storm</a
      >
      — that URL is the apt root, not the marketing site at storm.dewansh.space.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={bootstrap} label="install" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="update">
    <h2>Update</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Once the apt source is registered, upgrade through apt — there is no
      <code>storm-server upgrade</code>. That refreshes the binary and the web
      client under <code>/usr/share/storm/web</code>. Restart the unit so the
      new binary is what is listening; <code>/etc/storm/storm.env</code> is left
      alone across upgrades.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={upgrade} label="update" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="start">
    <h2>Start the server</h2>
    <p class="support">
      <code>up</code> creates the <code>storm</code> user when it can, writes
      <code>/etc/storm/storm.env</code> (mode 600), and enables the systemd
      unit. Default data root is <code>/srv/storm</code>. The web client is then
      at <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:{release.webPort}</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="support">
      <strong>There is no token in that file.</strong> Storm has no shared
      password and no cloud account — credentials are per device and live in
      <code>state/auth.db</code>, never in a config file. Getting the first
      device in is the next step.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={up} label="up" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="first-login">
    <h2>First login</h2>
    <p class="support">
      The first account on a server is always an <strong>owner</strong>.
      Everything after it — more devices, more accounts, MCP keys — happens from
      a client that is already signed in.
    </p>

    <h3>From a browser</h3>
    <p class="support">
      Open <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:{release.webPort}</code> on the same
      network. The page pairs itself with the server that served it, then asks
      you to create that first account. Nothing to run on the host.
    </p>

    <h3>From the macOS or Android app</h3>
    <p class="support">
      Print a single-use pairing code on the host and scan it in Storm.
      <code>pair</code> works only while the server has no accounts — after
      that, add a device from a client that is already signed in.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={pair} label="pair" />

    <h3>On the host</h3>
    <p class="support">
      Accounts are managed on the box too, which is the recovery path for a
      forgotten password. <strong>Run these as <code>storm</code>, not as
      root</strong> — <code>auth.db</code> is created on first use, so a
      root-owned database is one the service cannot write, and the symptom
      arrives much later as a login that fails with nothing useful in the
      journal.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={accounts} label="accounts" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow">
    <h2>Custom paths</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Point <code>--vault-root</code> at a directory that <em>contains</em>
      vaults (one folder per vault), not at a single vault. Vaults on NFS often
      need the process to run as a user that can write the mount —
      <code>up</code> follows the state-dir owner in that case.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={upCustom} label="up · custom" />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="clients">
    <h2>Clients</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Native builds for macOS and Android, plus the web UI served by your
      server. Downloads and checksums live on the
      <a href="/clients">Clients</a> page.
    </p>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/clients">Get a client →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow">
    <h2>MCP</h2>
    <p class="support">
      After the server is up, enable MCP from the client under Server → AI
      access, or via the config API — it is off by default, and write tools need
      writable mode. An MCP client authenticates with a <em>key</em> you mint in
      the app, shown once and revocable; it is accepted on <code>/mcp</code> and
      nowhere else. Details and the twelve tools are documented in
      <a
        href="https://github.com/dewanshDT/Storm/blob/main/apps/server/README.md"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">apps/server/README.md</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.92em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .section h3 {
    margin: calc(var(--sp) * 5) 0 calc(var(--sp) * 1.5);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .section h3:first-of-type {
    margin-top: calc(var(--sp) * 2);
  }

  .support em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .cta-row {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
